# Line endings are part of how this repository behaves, not a local preference.
#
# `core.autocrlf` is true on the machine this is developed on, so checkout wrote
# CRLF while git stored LF. That left a mixed working tree: a file git last wrote
# carried CRLF, a file a tool last wrote carried LF, and the same check could
# pass for one and fail for the other.
#
# Four of them broke on it in one day (19 September 2026). Three size budgets
# counted bytes off the disk, so they charged a file one byte per line for
# nothing and failed a 10,000-byte budget on a fresh clone while passing here.
# An exact-view test sliced a reference by offset after reading it in text mode,
# then compared the slice against the reader it was testing, which opens the same
# file with `newline=""` on purpose; five subtests failed against views that were
# correct. Each fault looked like the thing it was measuring rather than like a
# line ending, and each was invisible on the machine that introduced it.
#
# So text is LF everywhere, on disk as well as in the repository, and what a
# check reports no longer depends on who last wrote the file. Nothing stored
# changes: git already held LF, so this only settles what gets written out.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Media and archives are never line-ending converted, whatever the content
# detection decides about them. The library index is gzip; a single flipped byte
# makes it unreadable rather than merely wrong.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.pptx binary
*.docx binary
*.xlsx binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.gz binary
*.zip binary
